{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added the per-feed `refresh_interval` (integer minutes, 0 = use the global interval). The existing unit and integration tests pass; nothing exercises the new behaviour yet, as I added no tests.\n\nWhat was added:\n- **Migration:** new `feeds.refresh_interval int not null default 0` column.\n- **API:** the field appears in the feed object and is accepted by `PUT /v1/feeds/{feedID}`; the Go client models carry it too.\n- **Web UI:** the feed edit form has a number input named `refresh_interval`.\n- **Scheduler:** `ScheduleNextCheck` uses the per-feed value instead of the globally computed interval when it is above 0.\n\nBehaviour you might not assume:\n- **Server-requested delays still win when longer.** A TTL / Retry-After / Cache-Control delay longer than the per-feed interval is honoured, capped at the global maximum. The per-feed interval itself is not capped by the global maximum.\n- **Changing the interval reschedules the feed.** Setting a new non-zero value moves `next_check_at` to now + interval, so it takes effect immediately rather than after the previously scheduled check. Setting it back to 0 leaves the current schedule alone.\n- **Validation range is 0 to 525600 (one year).** The upper cap is my choice, to avoid overflow; negative or out-of-range values are rejected in both the API and the UI. A non-numeric value in the web form is rejected too; an empty one means 0.\n- **Not settable at feed creation.** Only the update endpoint and the edit form accept it; new feeds start at 0.\n- **Translations are English placeholders.** The label and error message were added to every locale file (required by the catalogue test), but only French is actually translated. The two keys sit together next to `no_media_player` rather than in alphabetical position.", "t": 1794461631.069}
{"tool": "_result", "name": "finish", "chars": 55, "t": 1794461631.077}
